  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 354 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over eastern Wyoming, with\nhigh stretching through Saskatchewan nudging into southwest\nManitoba. Upper level analysis places high over the desert \nsouthwest, with low amplitude ridging stretching northward, \nwhile upper low remains over Hudson Bay. Over our area, we \nremain in west-northwest flow, albeit a tad cyclonic. A few weak\nradar echos were recently over far north-central North Dakota, \nbut now have focused north of the international border.\n\nFor today, seasonal temperatures are expected with most\nlocations pushing into the 80s, with some 70s over far northwest\ninto north central North Dakota. With the surface low to the\nsouth-soutwest, a modest east-southeast wind is expected which\nwill usher in some higher low-level moisture. A few showers and\nthunderstorms may develop over parts of the area this morning\ninto mid-day as a modest wave moves through the weakly cyclonic\nflow, but better chances come later this afternoon and this\nevening primarily to southern locations as a short wave trough \nmoves through South Dakota nudging into North Dakota. Further\nnorth convection may be limited due to rather modest upper \nlevel height rises. With respect to severe potential, 0-6 km \nshear values remain quite elevated, at around 50kts, while \nlatest guidance indicates elevated instability with CAPE values \naround 2500 J/Kg, highest values along the South Dakota border.\nTherefore, large hail with some strong wind gusts will be \npossible, greatest potential along our southern areas. \n\nA similar scenario will be in place for Tuesday, though latest\nguidance is a little more aggressive with the strength of the\nshort wave moving through. Deep layer shear values remain\nelevated, with ample instability expected for additional severe\npotential, particularly over the southern half of the state.\n\nA gradual cooling trend then develops Wednesday through Friday.\nDaily chances for precipitation remain in the forecast with\nvarious short-wave troughs moving through, but a pattern shift\nappears to come either late Thursday or Friday as ridge axis\npushes east and flow briefly turns southwesterly. This will\nbring higher chances for precipitation to the area. For this \nweekend into early next week, modest chances for precipitation \nremain in the forecast, with a gradual warming trend possible.",
